    {
      "title": "Easy Solutions",
      "slug": "easy-solutions",
      "displayOrder": 2,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 7,
        "lessonOrder": 2
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "title": "Compare Stitch Patterns",
          "slug": "text-video-1782585048979",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 254,
            "blockType": "HTML",
            "editorLayout": "textVideo"
          },
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<p>Different stitch patterns produce different widths.</p><p>  Sometimes changing the stitch pattern will give you the width you need\n</p>\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 6163,
              "order": 1,
              "layoutRole": "textVideoLeft"
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151857740",
              "title": "For more width Consider a Different Stitch Pattern",
              "legacyComponentId": 6164,
              "order": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Note: Stitches and rows shown are for standard machine <em>(Mid-gauge/bulky)</em><br>\n\nIf you don't have an automatic patterning machine (punchcard or electronics)\n follow your manual for manually creating a tuck or Pull-up stitch<br><br>\n<b>Swatch 1:</b><br>\n<ol>\n<li>Cast on 60<i>(30)</i> stitches and knit in plain knitting for 60<i>(30)</i> rows</li>\n<li>Bind off loosely</li>\n</ol>\n<b>Swatch 2:</b><br>\n<ol><li>Cast on 60<i>(30)</i> stitches and knit in a tuck stitch knitting for 60<i style=\"font-size: 0.85rem;\">(30)</i><span style=\"font-size: 0.85rem;\"> rows</span></li><li>Bind off loosely</li></ol>\n\n\n<br>Compare and measure the 2 swatches - notice that both the width and length vary quite a bit.  Simply using a tuck stitch can sometimes solve the problem of not having enough needles to knit a piece.<div><br>\n\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/images/course-content/2/compare.jpg\">\n\n</div>",
              "legacyComponentId": 764,
              "order": 3
            }
          ],
          "title": "Stitch Pattern Practice",
          "slug": "stitch-pattern-practice",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 252,
            "blockType": "Practice"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "If you have a ribber, you can utilize both beds. This is also called \"U\" knitting.<br>\r\nBut remember, on Japanese machines automatic stitch patterning only occurs on the main bed. This solution will produce a wide piece of plain stockinette knitting. <br>You can use a textured or variegated yarn ... or even stripes to jazz up the plain knitting and create a wide knitted piece - get creative!",
              "legacyComponentId": 782,
              "order": 4
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859462",
              "title": "Half Circular U-Knitting",
              "legacyComponentId": 757,
              "order": 5
            }
          ],
          "title": "Half Circular Knitting with the Ribber",
          "slug": "half-circular-knitting-with-the-ribber",
          "order": 3,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 73,
            "blockType": "Video"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<b>Practice:</b><br>\nIf you have a ribber, following the video and your manual cast on 30 stitches on each bed and set your machine to knit half tubular (U knitting).<br>Once you get in the rhythm, the knitting isn't difficult and the advantage is a wide piece of knitting with no seams.<br>\n\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/images/course-content/2/half1.jpg\" width=\"500\">\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 766,
              "order": 6
            }
          ],
          "title": "Half Circular Practice",
          "slug": "half-circular-practice",
          "order": 4,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 253,
            "blockType": "Practice"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Decorative Seams",
      "slug": "decorative-seams",
      "displayOrder": 3,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 64,
        "lessonOrder": 3
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151856969",
              "title": "Hairpin Lace Seam",
              "legacyComponentId": 758,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<span style=\"color: rgb(43, 54, 54); font-family: &quot;Lucida Sans Unicode&quot;; font-size: 14px;\">A machine knitters staple, this join gives the appearance of hairpin lace or a cable between 2 pieces of knitting, adding a decorative touch.</span><p></p>",
              "legacyComponentId": 6165,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Hairpin Lace Seam",
          "slug": "hairpin-lace-seam",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 250,
            "blockType": "Video"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Follow the video to create 2 swatches that you will join with a hairpin seam.  It is helpful to use a contrasting color for each swatch, making it easier to work the join until you get the hang of it.<br><blockquote class=\"smallblock\"><small>Stitches and rows shown are for standard machine <em>(Mid-gauge/bulky)</em></small></blockquote>\n\n<b>Swatch 1:</b>\n<ul>\n<li>Cast on 60 <em>(30)</em> needles</li>\n<li>On the RIGHT side, leave a needle out of work as indicated in the video<br>\n<img src=\"/images/course-content/2/left.png\">\n</li>\n</ul>\n<br><b>Swatch 2:</b>\n<ul>\n<li>Cast on 60 <em>(30)</em> needles, on the LEFT side, leave a needle out of work as indicated in the video<br>\n<img src=\"/images/course-content/2/right.png\">\n</li>\n</ul>\nJoin the swatches following the video taking care to catch all strands and secure the stitches at the end.\n<br><br><span class=\"handwriting\">Does your swatch look like ours?</span><Br>\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/images/course-content/2/hairpin_intro.jpg\" width=\"500\"><br>\n\n<b>TIP:</b><br>Use this join to create belts and straps.  Knit a single piece, leaving a needle out of work on each edge.  Fold the band in half and work the Hairpin Lace technique to join.<br>\n\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/images/course-content/2/belt.jpg\" width=\"500\"></div>",
              "legacyComponentId": 783,
              "order": 3
            }
          ],
          "title": "Hairpin Lace Practice",
          "slug": "hairpin-lace-practice",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 257,
            "blockType": "Practice"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151858984",
              "title": "Show off Seams",
              "legacyComponentId": 771,
              "order": 4
            }
          ],
          "title": "Show off Seams",
          "slug": "show-off-seams",
          "order": 3,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 255,
            "blockType": "Video"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<img src=\"/images/course-content/2/practice_makes_perfect.png\"><br>Choose  your favorite technique from the video and knit 2 swatches, working the seam as shown.<br>The more you practice, the more likely you will remember to use these techniques to create your own designs.\n\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 786,
              "order": 5
            }
          ],
          "title": "Show off Seams Practice",
          "slug": "show-off-seams-practice",
          "order": 4,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 258,
            "blockType": "Practice"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859092",
              "title": "Twisted Join",
              "legacyComponentId": 775,
              "order": 6
            }
          ],
          "title": "Twisted Join",
          "slug": "twisted-join",
          "order": 5,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 256,
            "blockType": "Video"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Following the instruction in the video, practice the Twisted Join technique yourself.<br>\r\nIt knits up quickly and is easier than the Hairpin Lace technique.  The join has a cable appearance that is attractive when both pieces are knit in the same color or in contrasting colors.",
              "legacyComponentId": 788,
              "order": 7
            }
          ],
          "title": "Twisted Join Practice",
          "slug": "twisted-join-practice",
          "order": 6,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 259,
            "blockType": "Practice"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sew as you go",
      "slug": "sew-as-you-go",
      "displayOrder": 4,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 65,
        "lessonOrder": 4
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "title": "Quick Seams",
          "slug": "three-videos-1782587266499",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 261,
            "blockType": "HTML",
            "editorLayout": "threeVideosWithText"
          },
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<p>\n   Quickly \"seam\" knitted pieces as you knit.<br>\n\nThis technique is often used to quickly join blanket panels, eliminating the need to seam the panels when the knitting is complete.<br>\nIt can also be used to join other seams, cutting down on finishing time.\n</p>",
              "legacyComponentId": 6166,
              "order": 1,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosIntro"
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151856919",
              "title": "Watch How It's Done",
              "legacyComponentId": 6167,
              "order": 3,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosVideo1"
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "",
              "legacyComponentId": 6168,
              "order": 2,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosCaption1"
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "169798401",
              "title": "Sew-as-you-go Tricks",
              "legacyComponentId": 6169,
              "order": 5,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosVideo2"
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "",
              "legacyComponentId": 6170,
              "order": 4,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosCaption2"
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "",
              "title": null,
              "legacyComponentId": 6171,
              "order": 7,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosVideo3"
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "",
              "legacyComponentId": 6172,
              "order": 6,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosCaption3"
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "",
              "legacyComponentId": 6173,
              "order": 8,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosOutro"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<div>\n\n\nIf you have never tried Sew-as-you-go, please take time to try the technique.  Not only is it good for joining panels, you will find many other uses. Often socks knit on the flatbed knitting machine are seamed using this method. <br>It's a neat, attractive, strong seam, that SAVES TIME!<br>Follow the video as you are practicing. Use light color yarn (and contrasting colors if possible) so you can see the formation of the sew-as-you-go stitches.<br><b>Swatch 1:</b><br>\nKnit a small swatch or use the edge of a previously knit swatch for this sample.<br>\n<b>Swatch 2:</b><br>\n<ul>\n<li>Cast on a new swatch</li>\n<li>On the side opposite the carriage pick up and hang the cast on edge stitch of Swatch 1. Be sure to have the Private/wrong side of Swatch 1 facing you. (purl side facing on both pieces)</li>\n<li>Be sure to hang the stitch from swatch one ON THE SAME NEEDLE as the end needle of Swatch 2.  Don't increase, double up the stitches as you join to create the seam.</li>\n<li>To reduce bulk,  pick up a single strand (one leg) of the edge stitch</li>\n<li>Knit across and back (carriage is on the opposite side of the seam)</li>\n<li>Continue picking up stitches from Swatch 1 as you knit Swatch 2</li>\n</ul><br>\n<b>TIP:</b><br>\nPlacing yarn markers at regular intervals can be helpful in seaming with any method. For example, as you are knitting the first piece place a yarn marker along the edges every 10, 20 or 25 rows (whatever interval you will remember).<br>\nAs you knit the second piece and sew-as-you-go, the markers will help you match the rows of both pieces perfectly.&lt;br&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/images/course-content/2/sew_You_go2.png\">\n\n\n</div>",
              "legacyComponentId": 790,
              "order": 9
            }
          ],
          "title": "You HAVE to try this!",
          "slug": "you-have-to-try-this",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 260,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Pattern Hacking Practice",
      "slug": "lesson-1782588375955",
      "displayOrder": 5,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 66,
        "lessonOrder": 5
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "title": "Make it your own",
          "slug": "content-1782588426794",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 2,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          },
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Just because a pattern tells us to cast on the entire width of the back of a sweater, doesn't mean we have to do that.\n<br>\nOften with Knit it Now Dynamic Patterns, you will be instructed to cast on more stitches than you have available. (The engine does it's math thing, but doesn't know how many needles you have available.)<br>\n\nAs shown in the previous \"Not Enough Needles II\" video, we have options for adding seams.<br>\n\n<span class=\"handwriting-accent\">Let's do a little math and explore 5 possibilities.</span><br><br>\n\n<a href=\"/downloads/pattern_hack_exercise2.pdf\" class=\"kbm-btn kbm-btn-primary\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Download Pattern Hacking Practice</a>",
              "legacyComponentId": 6174,
              "order": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
